use ../../../lib/UiComponent

from ../../../behaviors/Selection import Selection
from ../../../behaviors/Traversal import Traversal
from ../../buttons/Button import Button
from ../Popover import Popover

export ui-component Menu:
    description:
        A menu is a list of actions that pops up from a button, such as "Edit,
        Duplicate, Delete" behind a "More" button. Picking an item does it and
        closes the menu. Items can also be options that are ticked on and off,
        and can open further menus of their own.

    composes:
        - @{Button}
        - @{Popover}

    behaviors:
        - @{Traversal}
        - @{Selection}

    opening:
        Pressing the button opens the menu. From the keyboard, Enter, Space or
        Down opens it with the first item current, and Up opens it with the
        last item current. The button tells assistive tools that it opens a
        menu, and whether it's open. Several buttons can share one menu.

    focus:
        While the menu is open, the keyboard works on the menu. Either focus
        moves onto the current item, or it stays on the menu while the current
        item is highlighted; either way assistive tools are told which item
        is current. Which of these is up to the chosen library. Pointing at an
        item makes it current too.

    moving:
        Up and Down move between items, and Home and End go to the first and
        last, as Traversal describes. Whether they wrap round at the ends is up
        to the component. Typing letters jumps to the next item starting with
        them. Disabled items can be landed on but not used. Group headings and
        separators are passed over.

    items:
        Each item has a label, and may have an icon and a hint of its keyboard
        shortcut. An item can do something, or take the user somewhere, like a
        Link. Pressing Enter or Space on the current item, or clicking an item,
        uses it. The component is told which item was used.

    closing:
        Using an action item closes the menu and puts focus back on the button,
        unless the component keeps the menu open. It also closes the ways
        Dismissal describes. Pressing Tab closes it and moves on from the
        button, as if it had never opened.

    groups:
        Items can be put in groups, each with a heading, and divided by
        separators. Headings and separators can't be used.

    checkboxItems:
        An item can be an option that is ticked or unticked, such as "Show
        grid". Using it flips it, independently of the others. It shows whether
        it's ticked, and tells assistive tools.

    radioItems:
        A group of items can be a set where one is picked, such as a sort order.
        Using one picks it and un-picks the one before, as Selection describes
        for picking one item. Using the picked one again leaves it picked.

    optionClosing:
        Whether using a ticked or picked item closes the menu is up to the
        component, so the user can change several at once.

    submenus:
        An item can open a further menu beside it, and shows that it does.
        Right, Enter or Space opens it and makes its first item current, and
        Left or Escape closes it and goes back to the item that opened it. For
        right-to-left languages, Left and Right swap. Pointing at the item
        opens it after a moment. Moving the pointer diagonally towards the
        open submenu, across other items, doesn't close it.

    submenuClosing:
        Using an action item anywhere closes every open menu at once. Escape
        closes only the innermost one. Clicking outside them all closes them
        all.

    nonGoals:
        - Not for picking a value that is then shown, like a country. That's a
            Select
        - Not a menu bar, the row of menus along the top of an application
        - Not a site's navigation. A list of links to other pages is a list of
            Links
